<p>In 1880, seven undergraduates at Balliol College, Oxford, published a broadsheet of 40 quatrains making fun of their masters. The college suppressed it, but a few copies survived, and librarian W.G. Hiscock published the collection in 1939:</p>
<p>First come I. My name is Jowett.<br />
There&#8217;s no knowledge but I know it.<br />
I am Master of this College,<br />
What I don&#8217;t know isn&#8217;t knowledge.</p>
<p>Here am I, the often sat on<br />
Dancing don; my name is Tatton;<br />
Like old wine in a new bottle<br />
Is my talk on Aristotle.</p>
<p>I am Huxley, blond and merry,<br />
Fond of jokes and laughter, very:<br />
If I laughed at what was witty,<br />
I should laugh less, which were pity.</p>
<p>&#8216;Tis not by feeding tea and shrimps on<br />
That you&#8217;d become as thin as Simpson:<br />
You might by trying to defer<br />
So obstinate a questioner.</p>
